d 'PSTRATA': module for optimal propensity score stratification
d
d  pstrata stratifies the propensity score into strata
d (quantile-based bins) chosen so that, within each stratum, the
d treatment groups are balanced on the propensity score. In
d general, stratification (also referred to as subclassification)
d allows the investigator to analyze outcomes between treatment
d groups within each stratum as well as to observe overall
d differences between groups across all strata (Linden & Adams
d 2008). It has been shown that stratification of the propensity
d score into five quantiles can remove over 90% of the initial bias
d due to the covariates used to create the propensity score
d (Cochran 1968; Rosenbaum & Rubin 1984). While the strata
d generated by pstrata can be used in any subsequent analysis, it
d was specifically intended for use in conjunction with mmws; which
d can be downloaded from SSC. Before using pstrata, the propensity
d score for a given treatment must be estimated. A logit or probit
d regression model can be used for estimating the propensity score
d for a binary treatment and a multinomial logistic or probit
d regression can be used for estimating propensity scores for
d multiple treatment levels. pstrata then generates one or more
d strata variables (named strata1, strata2, etc.), depending on the
d number of treatment groups in the study. 
d
d KW: propensity score
d KW: stratification
d KW: weighting
d KW: marginal mean weighting
d KW: causal inference
d
d Requires: Stata version 11
d
d Distribution-Date: 20260913
d
d Author: Ariel Linden, Linden Consulting Group, LLC
d Support: email alinden@@lindenconsulting.org
d
f pstrata.ado
f pstrata.sthlp
